Step 5 — Explore read coverage
Use Samtools to calculate the number of reads covering each reference position:
samtools depth -aa results/bam/SRR26396743.bam \
  &gt; results/bam/SRR26396743.depth.tsv
Calculate the mean depth across the reference, including positions with zero coverage:
awk '{sum += $3; n++} END {
  if (n &gt; 0) printf "Mean depth: %.2f\n", sum/n;
  else print "No positions found"
}' results/bam/SRR26396743.depth.tsv
Calculate the proportion of reference positions covered by at least one read:
awk '{total++; if ($3 &gt; 0) covered++}
END {
  if (total &gt; 0)
    printf "Reference covered: %.2f%%\n", 100*covered/total;
  else
    print "No positions found"
}' results/bam/SRR26396743.depth.tsv
Questions

What is the mean read depth?
What percentage of the reference is covered by at least one read?
Are there regions with zero coverage?
Why can a high mapping percentage coexist with low coverage in some regions?

Answers
Mapping percentage measures how many alignment records map to the reference. Coverage describes how many reads support each reference position.
A dataset may have a high mapping percentage while still containing poorly covered regions. Those regions may be unreliable for consensus generation or variant analysis.

A good figure

one message per figure, and the title states the message (not just “Phylogenetic tree”);
colour = a variable the reader cares about (region, lineage), with a legend;
label the sample(s) of interest; the caption gives the number of genomes and the method;
readable in grayscale and for colour-blind readers.

A one-page brief (PDF or doc) containing:

a key message of two sentences;
the data and quality section (genomes analysed / excluded, and why);
one figure (tree or timeline) with a self-explanatory caption;
an interpretation with an explicit confidence level;
limits and recommendations.

